2.34.1

We evaluated the adaptive-polling patch across 512 paired A/B cases on SOLIDIGM
PS1010 and Samsung PM9D3A NVMe SSDs. The matrix covered 4K random read/write
and 128K sequential read/write, using both libaio and io_uring, with single-drive and
four-drive configurations.

The patch showed 50 confirmed throughput improvements, with a median gain of 25.2%
and a maximum gain of 63.0%. Only five cases showed confirmed regressions; 411 cases
had no material change, while 46 cases—mostly random writes—were too variable to draw
a firm conclusion.

The benefit is concentrated in high-load 4K random-read workloads, especially multi-drive
configurations. In many read cases, IRQs per I/O were reduced by roughly 60–99% without
reducing throughput. The 128K workloads were generally throughput-neutral.
Overall, the patch provides a clear workload-dependent benefit, particularly where interrupt
processing limits 4K read performance. It is not a universal improvement: the Samsung libaio
QD33/QD34 random-read regressions and several P99 latency regressions still require
investigation.

Full test results:
https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1o-62qNXMrRJU93n2_uLItmeh7szipiIMMikLDMnsEe8/edit?gid=188992110#gid=188992110


